        int charsRead = scanf("%1023s\n", bufPtr);
        if (charsRead < 0) {
            // Error occurred, return -1
            return -1;
        } else if (charsRead == 1) {
            // Newline character read, break loop
            break;
        }
        bytesRead += charsRead;
        bufPtr += charsRead;
    }

    // Check for buffer overflow
    if (bytesRead >= 1023) {
        printf("Error: Buffer overflow\n");
        return -1;
    }

    return bytesRead;
}
```
Here's a brief explanation of the code:

1. The function takes a `char *buffer` as input, which will store the read string.
2. We use a `while` loop to repeatedly read from the standard input until we reach the end of the line (i.e., until we read a newline character).
3. Inside the loop, we use `scanf` with the format string `"1023s\n"` to read in at most 1023 characters and then the newline character.
4. We check if an error occurred (`charsRead < 0`) and return -1 if so.
5. If we successfully read some characters, we update our `bytesRead` counter and move our buffer pointer forward by the number of characters read.
6. Once we break out of the loop (i.e., when we read a newline character), we check if we exceeded the maximum allowed buffer size (1023 bytes). If so, we print an error message and return -1.

Note that this implementation assumes that the `scanf` format string is correct and does not perform any additional validation or error handling. In a real-world scenario, you would likely want to add more robust error handling and input validation.']

